/* Geodesic — extra written ("explain in words") questions, so every topic has at least two.
   Generated from tools/written_src.py by tools/build_written.py. Each: level, question, model answer, key ideas ("a|b" = alternatives). */
window.WL = window.WL || {};
WL.written = {"ks3-speed": [{"l": 1,"q": "Explain why the average speed for a whole journey is usually not the average of the speeds of its parts. Use an example.","a": "Average speed is total distance ÷ total time. The parts usually take different times, and the slow part lasts longer, so it counts for more. For example, 30 km at 30 km/h (1 h) then 30 km at 60 km/h (0.5 h) is 60 km in 1.5 h = 40 km/h, not 45 km/h.","k": ["total distance","total time","more time|longer time|takes longer|different times"]},{"l": 2,"q": "On a distance–time graph, what does a curved line that gets steeper mean, and how would you find the speed at one instant?","a": "The gradient of a distance–time graph is the speed, so a line that gets steeper means the speed is increasing: the object is accelerating. The speed at one instant is the gradient of the tangent drawn to the curve at that time (rise ÷ run of the tangent).","k": ["gradient|slope","speeding up|accelerating|acceleration|speed increases","tangent"]}],"ks3-moments": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why a wide, low-built bus is harder to tip over than a tall, narrow van.","a": "Weight acts through the centre of mass. A vehicle topples when the line of action of its weight passes outside its base, because then the weight's moment about the tipping edge turns it over instead of back. With a low centre of mass and a wide base it must tilt much further before that happens, so the bus is more stable.","k": ["centre of mass|center of mass|centre of gravity|center of gravity","outside the base|outside its base|beyond the base|past the edge","wide base|wider base|low|lower","moment|turning effect|turns"]}],"gcse-suvat": [{"l": 2,"q": "A ball is thrown straight up. At the very top of its flight, what are its velocity and its acceleration? Explain why many students get the acceleration wrong.","a": "At the top the velocity is zero for an instant, but the acceleration is still 9.8 m/s² downwards, because the ball's weight still acts on it. Students confuse “not moving” with “not accelerating”: acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, and at that moment the velocity is changing from upwards to downwards.","k": ["zero velocity|velocity is zero|velocity zero|v = 0|speed is zero","9.8|gravity|weight","downwards|downward|down","rate of change"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why the stopping distance of a car more than doubles when its speed doubles, even though the thinking distance only doubles.","a": "Thinking distance = speed × reaction time, so it is proportional to speed and doubles. Braking distance comes from the brakes doing work equal to the kinetic energy, F × d = ½mv², so for the same braking force it is proportional to v² and becomes four times larger. The total therefore more than doubles.","k": ["reaction time|thinking","kinetic energy|work done|½mv²","squared|square|v^2","four times|quadruple|4 times"]}],"gcse-energy": [{"l": 1,"q": "A ball is dropped and bounces back to a lower height each time. Has energy been destroyed? Explain where it went.","a": "No: energy is conserved. Each bounce transfers some of the ball's energy to the thermal store of the ball and floor, and some is carried away as sound, so less is left in the gravitational store at the top. The total is unchanged; it has been dissipated into less useful stores.","k": ["conserved|not destroyed|cannot be destroyed","thermal|heat|heating|internal","sound","dissipated|wasted|spread out|less useful"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the speed of a sledge at the bottom of a smooth slope does not depend on its mass.","a": "The gravitational potential energy lost, mgh, becomes kinetic energy, ½mv². The mass appears on both sides and cancels, so v = √(2gh): the speed depends only on the height dropped (if friction and air resistance are negligible).","k": ["potential energy|mgh|gravitational store","kinetic energy|½mv²","cancels|cancel","height"]}],"gcse-circuits": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why adding another resistor in parallel decreases the total resistance of a circuit.","a": "In parallel each branch gives the charge another path. The voltage across every branch is the same, so the old branches carry the same current as before and the new branch adds extra current. More total current for the same voltage means a smaller total resistance, R = V/I.","k": ["another path|extra path|more paths|another route|extra branch","same voltage|same potential difference|same p.d.","more current|total current|current increases","R = V/I|V = IR|resistance"]},{"l": 2,"q": "A filament lamp is not an ohmic conductor. Describe its I–V graph and explain its shape.","a": "The I–V graph is a curve through the origin that gets less steep as the current rises (an S shape for both directions). A larger current heats the filament; the metal ions vibrate more, the electrons collide with them more often, so the resistance increases and V/I is not constant.","k": ["curve|curved|not straight|less steep","temperature|heats|hotter|heating","resistance increases|resistance rises|higher resistance|more resistance","vibrate|collisions|collide"]}],"gcse-newton": [{"l": 2,"q": "A book rests on a table. Its weight and the normal force from the table are equal and opposite. Explain why they are NOT a Newton's third law pair.","a": "A third-law pair acts on two different objects and is the same type of force. The weight (Earth pulls the book) and the normal force (table pushes the book) both act on the book and are different types of force. They are equal because the book is in equilibrium (first law). The weight's partner is the book pulling the Earth up; the normal force's partner is the book pushing down on the table.","k": ["different objects|different bodies|two objects","same type|same kind","both act on the book|both on the book|same object","equilibrium|first law|balanced"]}],"al-circular": [{"l": 2,"q": "A passenger in a car taking a sharp bend feels “thrown outwards”. Explain what is really happening, as seen from the road (an inertial frame).","a": "No outward force acts. The passenger's body tends to keep moving in a straight line (Newton's first law). The car turns underneath them, so the seat or door must push them inwards to provide the centripetal force mv²/r towards the centre of the bend. The “outward push” is their own inertia, felt in the car's accelerating frame.","k": ["straight line|inertia","centripetal|towards the centre|toward the center","no outward force|no centrifugal|not a real force|fictitious","door|seat|friction|normal force"]}],"al-shm": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain, using a = −ω²x, why the period of a simple harmonic oscillator does not depend on its amplitude.","a": "The restoring acceleration is proportional to displacement. If the amplitude doubles, the acceleration at every corresponding point doubles, so the mass covers twice the distance at twice the speed, and each cycle takes the same time. Mathematically, x = A cos(ωt + φ) solves a = −ω²x for any A with the same ω, so T = 2π/ω.","k": ["proportional to displacement|proportional|a ∝ x","twice the distance|further|larger distance","faster|twice the speed|greater speed|higher speed","same ω|2π/ω|omega|ω"]}],"al-capacitors": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the discharge current of a capacitor through a resistor falls exponentially rather than at a steady rate.","a": "The current is I = V/R and V = Q/C, so the current is proportional to the charge left. As charge flows off, V falls and so does the current: the rate of loss is proportional to the amount remaining, dQ/dt = −Q/RC, which gives exponential decay with time constant RC.","k": ["proportional to the charge|proportional to Q|proportional","voltage falls|p.d. falls|V decreases|voltage decreases","rate|dQ/dt","time constant|RC"]}],"al-gravfields": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why gravitational potential is always negative, and what that tells you about a satellite in orbit.","a": "Potential is defined as zero at infinity, and gravity is attractive, so work must be done on a mass to take it out to infinity. Anywhere closer it therefore has less energy than at infinity: φ = −GM/r < 0. A satellite in orbit has negative total energy (−GMm/2r): it is bound and needs energy to escape.","k": ["zero at infinity|infinity","attractive","work must be done|work done|energy needed","bound|negative total energy|escape"]}],"al-efields": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why electric field lines always cross equipotential surfaces at right angles.","a": "Moving a charge along an equipotential involves no change in potential energy, so the field does no work on it for any step along the surface. The field therefore has no component along the surface: it must be perpendicular to it. Equivalently, E = −dV/dr points down the steepest potential gradient.","k": ["no work|zero work","no change in potential|constant potential|same potential","no component|perpendicular|right angles|90","gradient|steepest|−dV/dr"]}],"al-ideal-gas": [{"l": 2,"q": "Use kinetic theory to explain why the pressure of a gas in a sealed, rigid container rises when it is heated.","a": "Heating raises the temperature, so the mean kinetic energy of the molecules, (3/2)kT, increases and they move faster. They hit the walls more often and with a larger change of momentum per collision, so the force per unit area on the walls, the pressure, increases. The volume is fixed, so p ∝ T.","k": ["kinetic energy|faster|speed","more often|more frequent|frequency","change of momentum|change in momentum|momentum","force|pressure"]}],"y1-sr": [{"l": 2,"q": "Lightning strikes both ends of a moving train at the same moment according to an observer on the platform. Explain why a passenger at the middle of the train says the strikes were not simultaneous.","a": "Both observers measure the same speed of light. The passenger moves towards the light from the front strike and away from the light from the back, so the front flash reaches them first. In the train frame they sit at rest at the midpoint and light travels at c both ways, so they conclude the front strike happened first. Simultaneity of separated events depends on the frame.","k": ["speed of light|same c|constant c","front first|front flash|front strike","midpoint|middle","simultaneity|relative|depends on the frame"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Muons made high in the atmosphere live only about 2.2 μs at rest, yet many reach the ground. Explain this in the Earth's frame and in the muon's frame.","a": "In the Earth frame the fast muon's clock runs slow by γ, so it lives γ × 2.2 μs and can cover the 10–15 km to the ground. In the muon's frame its lifetime is the ordinary 2.2 μs, but the atmosphere rushes past and is length-contracted by 1/γ, so the distance is short enough. Both frames agree on what is observed: the muon reaches the ground.","k": ["time dilation|clock runs slow|dilated","length contraction|contracted|shorter","γ|gamma|lorentz","agree|same result|both frames"]}],"y1-rotation": [{"l": 2,"q": "A solid ball and a hollow ball with the same mass and radius roll from rest down the same slope. Which reaches the bottom first? Explain.","a": "The solid ball. Rolling shares the energy between translation (½mv²) and rotation (½Iω², with v = ωR). The hollow ball has its mass further from the axis, so its moment of inertia is larger (I/MR² = 2/3 against 2/5), more of its energy goes into spinning, and less is left for forward speed: v² = 2gh/(1 + I/MR²), independent of mass and radius.","k": ["solid","moment of inertia","rotation|rotational|spinning","further from the axis|mass distribution|distribution"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why a figure skater spins faster when she pulls her arms in, and whether her kinetic energy changes.","a": "No external torque acts about the spin axis, so her angular momentum L = Iω is conserved. Pulling her arms in reduces I, so ω increases. Her kinetic energy ½Iω² = L²/2I increases; the extra energy comes from the work her muscles do pulling her arms inwards.","k": ["angular momentum","conserved|conservation","moment of inertia|reduces I|smaller I|I decreases","work|muscles"]}],"y1-thermo": [{"l": 2,"q": "When a gas is compressed quickly (adiabatically), its temperature rises even though no heat is supplied. Explain using the first law.","a": "First law: ΔU = Q − W, with W the work done by the gas. Adiabatic means Q = 0. In a compression work is done on the gas, so W < 0 and ΔU = −W > 0. The internal energy rises, and for an ideal gas U depends only on T, so the temperature rises.","k": ["first law|ΔU = Q − W","no heat|Q = 0|adiabatic","work done on the gas|work done on|work","internal energy"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why no heat engine working between 600 K and 300 K can be more than 50% efficient, whatever its design.","a": "If an engine beat the Carnot efficiency 1 − T_c/T_h = 50%, it could drive a reversible Carnot engine backwards as a refrigerator, and the pair would move heat from cold to hot (or turn heat completely into work) with no other effect, which the second law forbids. Equivalently, the total entropy change −Q_h/T_h + Q_c/T_c must be ≥ 0, which gives W/Q_h ≤ 1 − T_c/T_h.","k": ["Carnot","1 - Tc/Th|1 − T_c/T_h|50%","second law","entropy|reversible"]}],"y1-qm-intro": [{"l": 2,"q": "In a double-slit experiment with electrons sent one at a time, each electron makes a single dot, yet an interference pattern builds up. Explain what this shows.","a": "Each detection is localised, like a particle, but where it lands follows the probability |ψ|² of a wave that went through both slits and interfered. The pattern is a probability distribution that appears only after many electrons. So each electron, even sent one at a time, propagates as a wave and is detected as a particle: wave–particle duality. It is not different electrons interfering with each other.","k": ["probability","both slits","wave|wavefunction","particle|dot|detected","one at a time|single electron|each other"]}],"y2-boltzmann": [{"l": 2,"q": "Use the Boltzmann factor to explain why almost all hydrogen atoms at room temperature are in their ground state.","a": "Populations follow n₂/n₁ = (g₂/g₁)e^{−ΔE/kT}. The first excitation energy of hydrogen is 10.2 eV, while kT at 300 K is about 0.026 eV, so ΔE/kT ≈ 400 and e^{−400} is fantastically small. Thermal energy is far too small to excite the atoms.","k": ["Boltzmann factor|exponential|e^","kT|0.026","10.2|excitation energy|energy gap","far larger|much larger|tiny|small"]}],"y3-equivalence": [{"l": 2,"q": "Use the equivalence principle to explain why light must bend in a gravitational field.","a": "Locally, a uniform gravitational field cannot be told apart from an accelerating frame. In a rocket accelerating upwards, a light beam sent across the cabin hits the far wall lower than it entered, because the cabin accelerates while the light crosses; inside, its path curves downwards. By the equivalence principle the same must happen in a gravitational field, so light bends.","k": ["equivalence principle|indistinguishable|cannot be told apart","accelerating|acceleration|rocket|lift","lower|curves down|bends","light|beam"]}],"y4-friedmann": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why radiation energy density falls as a⁻⁴ while matter density falls only as a⁻³ as the Universe expands.","a": "For both, the number of particles per unit volume falls as a⁻³, because volumes grow as a³. Matter particles keep their rest energy, so ρ_m ∝ a⁻³. Each photon is also redshifted: its wavelength stretches in proportion to a, so its energy hc/λ falls as 1/a, giving ρ_r ∝ a⁻⁴.","k": ["volume|number density","redshift|wavelength stretches|stretched","photon energy|energy per photon|hc/λ","rest energy|rest mass"]}],"m-laguerre": [{"l": 3,"q": "When solving hydrogen's radial equation, why must the power series terminate, and what has this to do with energy quantisation?","a": "If the series does not stop, its coefficients at large ρ behave like those of e^{ρ}, so u grows like e^{ρ/2} and the wavefunction cannot be normalised. Demanding a polynomial (the series stops at a finite power) imposes a condition tying the energy to an integer, n = n_r + l + 1, giving E_n = −13.6 eV/n². Normalisability is what quantises the energy.","k": ["normalisable|normalizable|normalise|normalize|blows up","terminate|polynomial|stops|finite","exponential|e^","integer|quantise|quantize|quantised|quantized"]}],"m-erf": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain the trick for evaluating ∫e^{−x²} dx over the whole real line, and why it works even though e^{−x²} has no elementary antiderivative.","a": "Square the integral and write it as a double integral over the plane: I² = ∬e^{−(x²+y²)} dx dy. In polar coordinates dx dy = r dr dθ, and the extra r makes the radial integral elementary: ∫₀^∞ e^{−r²} r dr = ½. So I² = 2π × ½ = π and I = √π. It works because the integral over all space has a rotational symmetry the indefinite integral lacks.","k": ["square|I²|I^2","double integral|plane|two dimensions","polar","√π|root pi|sqrt"]},{"l": 2,"q": "A result is 5σ away from the prediction. Explain what this says about the chance that it is a random fluctuation, and why physicists demand 5σ before claiming a discovery.","a": "If only Gaussian noise were present, a fluctuation at least 5σ high (one-sided) has probability about 2.9 × 10⁻⁷, roughly 1 in 3.5 million. Experiments search in many places (the look-elsewhere effect) and systematic errors are often underestimated, so a strict threshold keeps false discoveries rare. It is not the probability that the hypothesis is false.","k": ["Gaussian|normal|noise|fluctuation","2.9|10^-7|3.5 million|million","look-elsewhere|many places|many searches|trials","systematic"]}],"gcse-measurement": [{"l": 1,"q": "Explain why timing 10 swings of a pendulum and dividing by 10 gives a more accurate period than timing one swing.","a": "The main error is human reaction time, about 0.2 s when starting and stopping the timer, and it is the same whether you time one swing or ten. Spread over ten swings, the error in the period is divided by ten.","k": ["reaction time","same error|error|uncertainty","divided by ten|divided by 10|ten times smaller|smaller"]}],"gcse-mass-weight": [{"l": 1,"q": "An astronaut has a mass of 70 kg. Explain what happens to her mass and her weight on the Moon.","a": "Her mass stays 70 kg: it measures the amount of matter (and her inertia), which does not depend on where she is. Her weight W = mg drops to about one-sixth, because the Moon's gravitational field strength (about 1.6 N/kg) is about one-sixth of Earth's.","k": ["mass stays|mass is the same|mass unchanged|same mass","weight","gravitational field strength|field strength|g","one-sixth|1/6|1.6|smaller|less"]}],"gcse-forces": [{"l": 2,"q": "A skydiver falls at a steady terminal velocity. Explain, in terms of forces, why she is not speeding up even though gravity still pulls on her.","a": "Air resistance grows with speed. At terminal velocity the upward air resistance has grown until it equals her weight, so the resultant force is zero. With no resultant force there is no acceleration (F = ma), so her velocity stays constant.","k": ["air resistance|drag","equals|equal|balanced|balance","weight|gravity","resultant force|net force|no acceleration|zero acceleration"]}],"gcse-work-power": [{"l": 1,"q": "A waiter carries a tray across a level room at steady speed, holding it up. Explain why, in the physics sense, he does almost no work on the tray.","a": "Work = force × distance moved in the direction of the force. His force on the tray is upwards (it balances the tray's weight), but the tray moves horizontally, so it moves no distance along the force. No energy is transferred to the tray: its kinetic and potential energies do not change.","k": ["direction of the force|along the force","upwards|vertical|up","horizontal|horizontally","no energy|energy unchanged|no change in energy|no work"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Two students of weight 600 N climb the same stairs; one runs up in 5 s, the other walks up in 15 s. Compare the work they do and the power they develop.","a": "They do the same work, because work = weight × height gained depends on the force and the height, not on the time. The runner's power is three times larger, because power = work ÷ time and he does the same work in a third of the time.","k": ["same work|work is the same|equal work","height","power|rate","three times|3 times|triple"]}],"gcse-pressure": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why a dam is built much thicker at the bottom than at the top.","a": "Water pressure increases with depth (p = ρgh), so the water pushes hardest on the bottom of the dam. A thicker base is needed to withstand the larger force per unit area there; near the top the pressure is small.","k": ["depth|deeper","pressure increases|greater pressure|higher pressure|more pressure","ρgh|density","force|withstand"]}],"gcse-sound": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why sound cannot travel through space, but light can.","a": "Sound is a mechanical, longitudinal wave: particles of a medium vibrate and pass the vibration on as compressions and rarefactions. Space is a vacuum with (almost) no particles to vibrate. Light is an electromagnetic wave, oscillating electric and magnetic fields, which needs no medium.","k": ["medium|particles","vacuum|no particles","vibrate|vibration|compressions","electromagnetic"]}],"gcse-power-cost": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain whether a kilowatt-hour is a unit of power or of energy, and why electricity is sold in kWh rather than joules.","a": "A kilowatt-hour is a unit of energy: the energy used by a 1 kW appliance running for 1 hour, 1000 W × 3600 s = 3.6 MJ. The joule is tiny for household use, so kWh gives convenient numbers. The cost depends on the energy used, which is power × time.","k": ["energy","power × time|power and time|1 kW for 1 hour|kilowatt for an hour","3.6","small|tiny|convenient"]}],"al-dynamics": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why a heavy ball and a light ball dropped together (no air resistance) land at the same time, even though gravity pulls harder on the heavy one.","a": "The gravitational force is proportional to mass (W = mg), and so is the inertia that resists acceleration (F = ma). The acceleration a = W/m = mg/m = g is therefore the same for both: the heavy ball needs a bigger force to accelerate at the same rate, and it gets exactly that.","k": ["proportional to mass|W = mg|weight","inertia|F = ma","a = g|same acceleration|cancels"]}],"al-momentum": [{"l": 2,"q": "A gun recoils when it fires. Explain why, and why the bullet has far more kinetic energy than the gun even though their momenta are equal in size.","a": "Before firing the total momentum is zero; momentum is conserved, so afterwards the bullet's forward momentum is matched by the gun's equal backward momentum. Since KE = p²/2m, with equal p the much lighter bullet has far more kinetic energy (in the ratio of the masses).","k": ["momentum is conserved|conservation of momentum|conserved","zero|initially zero","equal and opposite|opposite|backward","p²/2m|lighter|mass"]}],"al-equilibrium": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why, for an object in equilibrium, you may take moments about any point you like, and why choosing the point cleverly helps.","a": "If the resultant force is zero, the total moment is the same about every point (moving the pivot by d changes the total moment by d × ΣF = 0). So if moments balance about one point, they balance about all. Taking moments about the point where an unknown force acts removes that force from the equation, because its moment arm is zero.","k": ["no resultant force|resultant force is zero|net force|ΣF = 0","any point|every point|all points","unknown force|eliminate|removes","moment arm|zero distance|passes through"]}],"al-energy": [{"l": 2,"q": "A satellite in a circular orbit moves at constant speed while gravity pulls on it all the time. Explain why gravity does no work on it.","a": "Work = Fs cos θ. In a circular orbit the gravitational force points to the centre, perpendicular to the velocity, so θ = 90° and cos θ = 0. No work is done, so the kinetic energy and speed stay constant; gravity only changes the direction of motion.","k": ["perpendicular|right angles|90","cos θ|cos 90","no work|zero work","direction|kinetic energy constant|speed constant"]}],"al-bfields": [{"l": 2,"q": "A charged particle moves in a uniform magnetic field. Explain why its speed never changes and why its path is a circle.","a": "The magnetic force F = BQv is always perpendicular to the velocity, so it does no work and the kinetic energy and speed stay constant. A force of constant size that is always perpendicular to the velocity is a centripetal force, so the particle moves in a circle with BQv = mv²/r, giving r = mv/BQ.","k": ["perpendicular","no work","constant speed|speed constant|kinetic energy constant|speed is constant","centripetal"]}],"al-photons": [{"l": 2,"q": "In the photoelectric effect, explain why a dim ultraviolet lamp ejects electrons from zinc but a very bright red lamp does not.","a": "Light arrives as photons of energy E = hf, and each electron absorbs one photon. To escape, that one photon must supply at least the work function. UV photons have a high enough frequency; red photons do not, however many arrive. Brightness only changes the number of photons per second, so it changes how many electrons are emitted, not whether emission happens.","k": ["photon","hf|frequency","work function","one photon|single photon|one electron","intensity|brightness|number of photons"]}],"al-spectra": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the dark lines in the absorption spectrum of a cool gas are at exactly the same wavelengths as the bright lines in its emission spectrum.","a": "Both come from transitions between the same discrete energy levels. A photon is absorbed only if hf matches the gap between two levels, which removes that wavelength from white light (a dark line). An excited atom dropping across the same gap emits a photon of the same energy, so the same wavelength appears as a bright line.","k": ["energy levels|discrete","energy gap|difference|hf = ΔE","same gap|same transitions|same energy","absorbed|emitted"]}],"y1-angular-momentum": [{"l": 3,"q": "A spinning bicycle wheel supported at one end of its axle does not fall but slowly swings round (precesses). Explain using τ = dL/dt.","a": "Gravity exerts a torque about the support, τ = r × W, which is horizontal and perpendicular to L. Torque is the rate of change of angular momentum, so dL = τ dt is horizontal and perpendicular to L: L changes direction, not size, and the axle swings round instead of dropping. The precession rate is Ω = τ/L = mgr/Iω.","k": ["torque","rate of change|dL/dt|change in angular momentum","perpendicular|horizontal","direction|precess","mgr|τ/L"]}],"y1-fluids": [{"l": 2,"q": "Use Bernoulli's equation to explain why the pressure in a horizontal pipe is lower where the pipe is narrower.","a": "By continuity Av is constant, so the fluid speeds up in the narrow section. Bernoulli's equation says p + ½ρv² + ρgz is constant along a streamline; with z unchanged, a larger ½ρv² must be matched by a smaller pressure. Physically, fluid only accelerates into the narrow part if the pressure behind it is higher than the pressure ahead.","k": ["continuity|Av","faster|speeds up|speed increases","Bernoulli|½ρv²","lower pressure|pressure decreases|pressure falls|pressure drops"]}],"y1-magnetostatics": [{"l": 2,"q": "Ampère's law ∮B·dl = μ₀I_{enc} holds for any steady current, yet it only lets you calculate B easily in a few cases. Explain why.","a": "The law gives one integral, not B at each point. To take B out of the integral you need a loop on which B has constant size and is parallel (or perpendicular) to dl, which needs high symmetry: an infinite straight wire, an ideal solenoid or a toroid. Without that symmetry the integral cannot be inverted and you need Biot–Savart.","k": ["symmetry","constant|same size|same magnitude","integral|out of the integral","Biot–Savart|Biot-Savart|Biot"]}],"y1-faraday": [{"l": 2,"q": "A magnet dropped through a copper tube falls much more slowly than through a plastic one. Explain using Faraday's and Lenz's laws.","a": "As the magnet falls, the flux through each ring of the copper tube changes, inducing an emf (Faraday) and eddy currents. By Lenz's law these currents make a magnetic field that opposes the change: it repels the magnet from below and attracts it from above, giving an upward force. The magnet's energy is dissipated as heat in the copper. Plastic is an insulator, so no current flows.","k": ["flux|changing flux","eddy currents|induced current|current","oppose|Lenz","insulator|plastic","heat|dissipated|thermal"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why the electric field induced by a changing magnetic field is not conservative, and what that means for “voltage” in such a circuit.","a": "Faraday's law gives ∮E·dl = −dΦ/dt ≠ 0, so the work done round a closed loop is not zero; equivalently ∇ × E = −∂B/∂t ≠ 0. A field with non-zero curl cannot be the gradient of a scalar potential, so the “voltage” between two points depends on the path. Two voltmeters on the same two points, with leads on opposite sides of the changing flux, can read different values.","k": ["closed loop|loop|∮","curl|∇ × E","potential|gradient","path|depends on the path|path-dependent"]}],"y1-optics": [{"l": 2,"q": "A soap film looks black just before it bursts. Explain why, considering the phase changes on reflection.","a": "Light reflected at the front surface (air to soap, low to high index) gains a phase shift of π; light reflected at the back surface (soap to air) does not. As the film becomes much thinner than a wavelength, the extra path 2nt tends to zero, leaving only the π shift, so the two reflections cancel for every colour: destructive interference, and the film looks black.","k": ["phase shift|phase change|π|half a wavelength","path difference|2nt|thickness|thin","destructive|cancel","every colour|all colours|all wavelengths"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why a diffraction grating gives much sharper bright lines than a double slit with the same slit spacing.","a": "Both have maxima where d sin θ = mλ and all slits are in phase. With N slits, moving slightly away from a maximum makes the many contributions fall out of phase quickly; they cancel completely once the phases wrap round a full circle across the N slits, at an angle about 1/N of the gap between maxima. More slits give narrower, brighter lines.","k": ["many slits|N slits|more slits","in phase","cancel|destructive","narrow|sharp|1/N"]}],"y1-entropy": [{"l": 2,"q": "A hot block at T_h and a cold block at T_c touch, and heat Q flows between them. Show that the total entropy increases.","a": "The hot block loses entropy Q/T_h and the cold one gains Q/T_c. Since T_c < T_h, Q/T_c > Q/T_h, so the total change ΔS = Q/T_c − Q/T_h > 0. Heat flowing the other way would reduce the total entropy, which the second law forbids for an isolated system.","k": ["Q/T|Q/T_c|Q/T_h","colder|lower temperature|T_c < T_h","total|net","second law|increase|increases"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why entropy is a state function even though heat is not.","a": "The heat absorbed between two states depends on the path taken. But for reversible paths the integral of δQ/T is the same for all of them: 1/T is an integrating factor that turns the inexact δQ into an exact differential dS (this follows from ∮δQ_rev/T = 0 for any reversible cycle, the Carnot result). So ΔS depends only on the end states.","k": ["path|depends on the path","reversible","integrating factor|1/T|δQ/T","cycle|∮|closed"]}],"y1-old-quantum": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain how Planck's hypothesis that energy comes in steps of hf removes the ultraviolet catastrophe.","a": "Classically every mode of the cavity has mean energy kT, and the number of modes grows as f², so the predicted energy density grows without limit at high frequency. If a mode's energy can only change in steps of hf, a high-frequency mode with hf ≫ kT is very unlikely to be excited at all (Boltzmann factor e^{−hf/kT}), so its mean energy falls towards zero and the spectrum is cut off.","k": ["kT|equipartition","modes|number of modes","hf ≫ kT|high frequency|large hf","Boltzmann|unlikely|exponentially"]}],"y2-step-barrier": [{"l": 2,"q": "A particle with E > V₀ meets a potential step and is partly reflected. Explain why this is surprising classically and what causes it quantum mechanically.","a": "Classically, a particle with more energy than the step always passes over it, just slowing down. Quantum mechanically, the wave has wavenumber k₁ before the step and a smaller k₂ after it. ψ and dψ/dx must both be continuous at the step, and with k₁ ≠ k₂ that cannot be done with a transmitted wave alone, so a reflected wave is needed: R = ((k₁ − k₂)/(k₁ + k₂))². Any abrupt change in a medium reflects waves.","k": ["classically|classical","wavenumber|wavelength changes|k₁ ≠ k₂","continuous|boundary conditions|matching","reflected wave|reflection|reflects"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why a one-dimensional attractive delta-function well has exactly one bound state.","a": "Away from the delta a bound state must decay as e^{−κ|x|}. The delta makes dψ/dx jump by −(2mα/ħ²)ψ(0), so −2κ = −2mα/ħ² and κ = mα/ħ² is fixed, giving E = −ħ²κ²/2m = −mα²/2ħ². An odd state vanishes at the delta and feels no attraction, and the even state has only this one shape (no nodes), so there is just one solution.","k": ["decay|exponential","jump|kink|discontinuity|derivative","fixed|one value|κ = mα/ħ²","odd|even|nodes"]}],"y2-matter-fields": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why it is useful to define D = ε₀E + P, and why ∇·D = ρ_f rather than ρ.","a": "In a dielectric there are free charges, which we control, and bound charges from the polarisation, ρ_b = −∇·P. Gauss's law ε₀∇·E = ρ_f + ρ_b involves the bound charges, which we do not know in advance. Moving them to the left gives ∇·(ε₀E + P) = ρ_f, so D is fixed by the free charge alone.","k": ["bound charge|bound charges","free charge","polarisation|polarization|−∇·P","Gauss"]}],"y3-hydrogen": [{"l": 2,"q": "The 1s radial probability density peaks at r = a₀ even though |ψ|² is largest at r = 0. Explain.","a": "|ψ|² is probability per unit volume, largest at the nucleus. The probability of finding the electron between r and r + dr is |ψ|² × 4πr² dr: the volume of that thin shell is zero at r = 0 and grows as r², while |ψ|² decays as e^{−2r/a₀}. Their product, ∝ r²e^{−2r/a₀}, is largest at r = a₀.","k": ["per unit volume|density","shell|4πr²","product|r²e","maximum|peaks|largest"]}],"y3-identical": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain how the antisymmetry of fermion wavefunctions leads to the Pauli exclusion principle.","a": "For identical fermions ψ(1, 2) = −ψ(2, 1). If both were in the same single-particle state φ, the antisymmetrised state would be φ(1)φ(2) − φ(2)φ(1) = 0: it vanishes, and zero is not a physical state. So no two identical fermions can occupy the same quantum state.","k": ["antisymmetric|exchange|swap|sign","same state|same quantum state","zero|vanishes|cancels"]}],"y3-fine-structure": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain the physical origin of spin–orbit coupling in hydrogen.","a": "In the electron's rest frame the proton circles it, producing a magnetic field proportional to the orbital angular momentum L. The electron's spin magnetic moment has energy −μ·B in that field, so the energy depends on how S is oriented relative to L: H_SO ∝ L·S/r³ (with the Thomas factor ½). States with j = l + ½ and j = l − ½ then have different energies.","k": ["rest frame|electron's frame","magnetic field","magnetic moment|spin","L·S|orientation","Thomas"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why the Lamb shift between 2s₁/₂ and 2p₁/₂ cannot come from the Dirac equation, and what does explain it.","a": "The Dirac equation gives energies that depend only on n and j, so 2s₁/₂ and 2p₁/₂ are exactly degenerate. The measured splitting of about 1058 MHz comes from quantum electrodynamics: the electron's interaction with vacuum fluctuations of the electromagnetic field (its self-energy), with a smaller contribution from vacuum polarisation. The 2s electron, which has density at the nucleus, is shifted up most.","k": ["degenerate|n and j|same j","QED|quantum electrodynamics","vacuum fluctuations|self-energy|vacuum","vacuum polarisation|vacuum polarization"]}],"y3-fluid-dynamics": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain what the Reynolds number measures, and why a scale model should be tested at the real flow's Reynolds number.","a": "Re = ρUL/μ is the ratio of inertial to viscous forces. Written in dimensionless variables, the incompressible Navier–Stokes equations contain only Re, so geometrically similar flows with the same Re have the same solutions in scaled units (dynamic similarity). For ships, wave drag also depends on the Froude number, and the two cannot usually be matched at once.","k": ["inertia|inertial","viscous|viscosity","ratio","dimensionless|similarity|same equations","Froude"]}],"y3-semiconductors": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the resistance of a pure semiconductor falls as it gets hotter, while a metal's rises.","a": "In an intrinsic semiconductor, carriers must be thermally excited across the band gap, so their number n_i ∝ e^{−E_g/2kT} rises steeply with temperature; this outweighs the extra scattering, so the resistance falls. A metal already has a fixed, huge number of free electrons; heating only increases lattice vibrations and scattering, so its resistance rises.","k": ["band gap","carriers|excited|number of electrons","exponential|exponentially|e^","scattering|vibrations|collisions"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain how a depletion region forms at a p–n junction and why it stops growing.","a": "Electrons diffuse from the n side into the p side, and holes the other way, where they recombine. This leaves uncovered positive donor ions on the n side and negative acceptor ions on the p side, a region with no free carriers. These fixed charges set up an electric field (the built-in potential) that drives carriers back; equilibrium is reached when this drift balances the diffusion.","k": ["diffuse|diffusion","recombine|recombination","ions|fixed charges","electric field|built-in potential","drift|balance|equilibrium"]}],"y4-renormalisation": [{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why the effective electromagnetic coupling α increases at higher energies (shorter distances).","a": "The vacuum contains virtual electron–positron pairs that are polarised around a charge, partly screening it, as a dielectric would. At long distances you see the screened charge, α ≈ 1/137. Probing at higher momentum, i.e. shorter distance, penetrates the cloud and sees more of the bare charge, so α grows (logarithmically), to about 1/128 at the Z mass.","k": ["virtual pairs|virtual|electron-positron|electron–positron","screening|screened|screen","shorter distance|closer|higher energy","1/128|grows|logarithm"]}],"y4-standard-model": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the weak force is short-ranged while electromagnetism is long-ranged, even though they are unified.","a": "The W and Z bosons are massive (about 80 and 91 GeV) through the Higgs mechanism, while the photon is massless. A virtual boson of mass M can exist only for a time of about ħ/Mc², so its range is about ħ/Mc ~ 10⁻¹⁸ m (a Yukawa potential e^{−r/λ}/r). The massless photon gives a 1/r potential with infinite range. At energies above about 100 GeV the two forces become comparable.","k": ["massive|mass","Higgs","massless","range|ħ/Mc|Yukawa"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why the Standard Model treats left-handed and right-handed fermions differently.","a": "Experiments (Wu's cobalt-60 decay, the measured helicity of neutrinos) show that the weak charged current acts only on left-handed particles: parity is maximally violated. So left-handed fermions sit in SU(2) doublets and right-handed ones in singlets with no SU(2) charge. That also forbids ordinary mass terms, which couple left to right, so fermion masses must come from Yukawa couplings to the Higgs.","k": ["parity","doublet|doublets|SU(2)","singlet|singlets","Wu|cobalt|helicity|experiment","mass terms|Yukawa|Higgs"]}],"y4-ising": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the one-dimensional Ising model has no phase transition at any temperature above zero.","a": "In 1D, flipping all the spins beyond some point costs only a fixed energy 2J (one broken bond), but the domain wall can sit in about N places, giving entropy k ln N. The free energy change ΔF = 2J − kT ln N is negative for large N at any T > 0, so domain walls multiply and destroy long-range order.","k": ["domain wall|domain","2J|fixed energy|finite energy|broken bond","entropy|ln N","free energy|ΔF"]},{"l": 3,"q": "State the main assumption of mean-field theory for the Ising model and explain why it gives wrong critical exponents in two and three dimensions.","a": "Mean-field theory replaces each spin's neighbours by the average magnetisation m, ignoring fluctuations and correlations. Near T_c the correlation length diverges and fluctuations become large and long-ranged, so the approximation fails; it is exact only above the upper critical dimension, d = 4. In 2D the exact β is 1/8, not ½.","k": ["average|mean field|mean magnetisation|mean magnetization","fluctuations|correlations","correlation length|diverges","upper critical dimension|d = 4|four dimensions"]}],"y4-phase-transitions": [{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why a liquid–gas critical point and a uniaxial magnet have the same critical exponents (universality).","a": "Near a critical point the correlation length diverges, so behaviour is dominated by fluctuations on scales far larger than any microscopic detail. Under renormalisation-group coarse-graining the microscopic parameters are irrelevant and flow away; what survives is set by the dimension, the symmetry of the order parameter and the range of the interactions. Both systems have a scalar order parameter with up–down (Z₂) symmetry in 3D, so they flow to the same fixed point: the 3D Ising class.","k": ["correlation length|diverges","renormalisation group|renormalization group|coarse-grain|coarse-graining","fixed point","dimension","symmetry|order parameter"]}],"y4-superconductivity": [{"l": 2,"q": "Use the Meissner effect to explain why a superconductor is more than a “perfect conductor”.","a": "A perfect conductor (R = 0) would only keep the magnetic flux inside it constant (dB/dt = 0): cooled in a field, it would trap that field. A superconductor expels the field when cooled below T_c in a field (B = 0 inside, apart from a thin London layer), whatever its history. So superconductivity is a distinct thermodynamic state, not just zero resistance.","k": ["perfect conductor|zero resistance","expels|expel|Meissner","trap|flux constant|history","thermodynamic state|state|phase"]}],"y4-quantum-info": [{"l": 2,"q": "n qubits have 2ⁿ complex amplitudes. Explain why this does not let a quantum computer read out 2ⁿ answers at once.","a": "Measuring collapses the state and gives only n classical bits, with probabilities set by |amplitude|²; the other amplitudes are lost (the Holevo bound limits n qubits to n bits of retrievable information). Quantum algorithms work by arranging interference so that wrong answers cancel and the right one is likely before the measurement.","k": ["measurement|measure","collapse|collapses","n bits|n classical bits|Holevo","interference|cancel"]}],"y4-schwarzschild": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain the difference between the event horizon at r = 2GM/c² and the singularity at r = 0 of a Schwarzschild black hole.","a": "At r = 2GM/c² the Schwarzschild coordinates misbehave, but curvature invariants (such as the Kretschmann scalar) stay finite: it is a coordinate singularity, removed by other coordinates (Eddington–Finkelstein, Kruskal), and a freely falling observer crosses it without noticing anything locally. It is a one-way surface. At r = 0 the curvature itself becomes infinite: a physical singularity with infinite tidal forces.","k": ["coordinate singularity|coordinates","curvature|Kretschmann|invariant","infinite|diverges","freely falling|falls through|crosses","one-way|cannot escape|no return"]},{"l": 3,"q": "A distant observer watches a probe fall towards a black hole. Explain why she never sees it cross the horizon, while the probe crosses in a finite time of its own.","a": "Light from the probe has to climb out of an ever deeper well: the gravitational redshift factor 1/√(1 − r_s/r) diverges as r → r_s, so the time between the signals she receives grows without limit and the image freezes and fades out exponentially (on a time-scale of order r_s/c). But the probe's own proper time to reach r_s is finite: locally the horizon is not a special place.","k": ["redshift","diverges|infinite|freezes","proper time","finite","light|signals"]}],"y4-early-universe": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain the horizon problem and how inflation solves it.","a": "The CMB has the same temperature to 1 part in 10⁵ across the whole sky, yet in a decelerating Big Bang, regions more than about 2° apart on the sky were never in causal contact before recombination, so nothing could have evened them out. Inflation is an early period of accelerating expansion (at least about 60 e-folds) that stretched one small, causally connected region to cover the whole observable universe, so it started uniform.","k": ["same temperature|uniform","causal contact|causally|never in contact","accelerating|exponential expansion|accelerated","small region|stretched"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the helium mass fraction from Big Bang nucleosynthesis comes out at about 25%.","a": "Neutron–proton conversions freeze out at about 1 s, when kT ≈ 0.8 MeV, leaving n/p ≈ e^{−Δmc²/kT} ≈ 1/5; free neutron decay then lowers this to about 1/7 by the time deuterium survives (a few minutes). Almost all the remaining neutrons end up in helium-4, so Y ≈ 2(n/p)/(1 + n/p) = 2(1/7)/(8/7) = 0.25.","k": ["freeze out|freezes|freeze-out","n/p|neutron to proton|neutron-to-proton","1/7","neutron decay|decay","helium-4|helium"]}],"m-graphs": [{"l": 1,"q": "Explain what the gradient of a distance–time graph means physically, and why a gradient has units.","a": "The gradient is rise ÷ run, here change in distance ÷ change in time, so it is the rate of change of distance with time: the speed. Because it divides metres by seconds it has units of m/s. A gradient always has units of “y per unit x”.","k": ["rate of change|rate","rise|change in","speed|velocity","units|m/s"]}],"m-trig": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 for every angle θ.","a": "On the unit circle the point at angle θ is (cos θ, sin θ). Its distance from the origin is 1, so by Pythagoras cos²θ + sin²θ = 1. This holds for every θ, including angles where sin or cos is negative, because squaring removes the sign.","k": ["unit circle","Pythagoras","(cos θ, sin θ)|coordinates","radius 1|distance 1|hypotenuse"]}],"m-proportion": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why an ant can lift many times its own weight, but an ant scaled up to the size of a horse would collapse.","a": "Strength depends on the cross-sectional area of muscles and legs, which scales as L², while weight depends on volume, which scales as L³. Scaling up by a factor λ multiplies strength by λ² but weight by λ³, so strength per unit weight falls by 1/λ. A horse-sized ant would be relatively far weaker and its legs could not hold it up.","k": ["area|cross-section","volume","squared|square|l^2","cubed|cube|l^3","ratio|relatively|per unit weight"]}],"m-fractions": [{"l": 2,"q": "A price rises by 10% and then falls by 10%. Explain why it does not return to the original price.","a": "Percentage changes multiply: × 1.10 then × 0.90 gives × 0.99, an overall drop of 1%. The 10% fall is taken from the larger, raised price, so it removes more than the 10% rise added.","k": ["multiply|multiplier","1.1","0.9","0.99|1%","larger|bigger|new price|raised price"]}],"m-indices": [{"l": 2,"q": "Use the rule aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = a^{m−n} to explain why a⁰ = 1 and a⁻¹ = 1/a.","a": "aⁿ ÷ aⁿ = 1, since anything non-zero divided by itself is 1, and by the rule it equals a^{n−n} = a⁰, so a⁰ = 1 (for a ≠ 0). Similarly a⁰ ÷ a¹ = a^{0−1} = a⁻¹, and a⁰ ÷ a = 1/a, so a⁻¹ = 1/a. The definitions are chosen so that the index laws keep working.","k": ["divided by itself|itself","index laws|the rule|consistent","a ≠ 0|non-zero|not zero","1/a"]}],"m-estimation": [{"l": 2,"q": "A length is 4.6 cm to the nearest millimetre. Explain what its bounds are, and why the upper bound is written as 4.65 even though 4.65 itself would round to 4.7.","a": "To the nearest millimetre means the true value is within half a millimetre: 4.55 ≤ L < 4.65. The value 4.65 itself is not included (it would round up), but every number just below it is, so 4.65 is the least upper bound and is the value used in calculations.","k": ["half|0.05","4.55","4.65","not included|strictly less|least upper bound"]}],"m-rates": [{"l": 2,"q": "One bank account adds £50 each year; another adds 5% each year. Explain the difference between these two kinds of growth.","a": "Adding £50 each year increases the balance by the same amount every year: linear growth, N = N₀ + 50n. Adding 5% multiplies the balance by 1.05 each year: exponential growth, N = N₀ × 1.05ⁿ, so the yearly increase itself grows as the balance grows. In the long run the exponential account overtakes any linear one.","k": ["same amount|linear|constant amount","multiply|1.05|multiplier","exponential|compound","overtakes|grows faster|eventually"]},{"l": 2,"q": "A car travels 60 km at 60 km/h and then 60 km at 20 km/h. Explain why its average speed is not 40 km/h.","a": "Average speed = total distance ÷ total time. The first part takes 1 h and the second 3 h, so it is 120 km in 4 h = 30 km/h. More time is spent at the slow speed, so the average is weighted towards it; averaging the two speeds wrongly gives them equal weight.","k": ["total distance","total time","30","more time|weighted|longer"]}],"m-equations": [{"l": 2,"q": "Use graphs to explain why the simultaneous equations y = 2x + 1 and y = 2x + 5 have no solution.","a": "Each equation is a straight line, and a solution is a point on both lines, an intersection. Both lines have gradient 2 but different intercepts, so they are parallel and never meet. Algebraically, subtracting gives 0 = 4, a contradiction.","k": ["parallel","gradient|slope","intersection|intersect|meet","contradiction|0 = 4"]}],"m-inequalities": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why you must reverse the inequality sign when you multiply both sides by a negative number. Give an example.","a": "Multiplying by a negative number reflects the number line through 0, which reverses the order of numbers. For example 2 < 5, but multiplying by −1 gives −2 and −5, and −2 > −5. So if x > 3, then −x < −3.","k": ["reverse|flip|reverses","order|number line","negative","example|2 < 5|−2 > −5"]}],"m-sequences": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain how differences tell you whether a sequence is linear or quadratic, and why a quadratic has a constant second difference.","a": "A linear sequence an + b has a constant first difference, a. For a quadratic an² + bn + c the first differences are a(2n + 1) + b, which themselves go up by 2a each time, so the second difference is constant and equal to 2a.","k": ["first difference|constant difference","second difference","2a","linear|quadratic"]}],"m-mensuration": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the area of a circle is πr², by cutting it into thin sectors.","a": "Cut the circle into many thin sectors and arrange them alternately point-up and point-down. They make a shape close to a parallelogram (almost a rectangle) of height r and length half the circumference, πr. Its area is about r × πr = πr², and the approximation becomes exact as the sectors get thinner.","k": ["sectors","rectangle|parallelogram","half the circumference|πr","height|radius"]}],"m-trig-rules": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the sine rule can give two different triangles when you know two sides and an angle that is not between them (the ambiguous case).","a": "Solving sin B = (b sin A)/a gives a value between 0 and 1, and two angles between 0° and 180° have that sine: B and 180° − B. If both make an angle sum less than 180°, both triangles exist. Geometrically, the side opposite the known angle can swing to meet the base line at two points.","k": ["two angles|two values|two solutions|two triangles","180° − B|180 − B|supplementary","angle sum|less than 180","swing|two points"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the cosine rule becomes Pythagoras' theorem when the angle is 90°, and what the extra term means otherwise.","a": "a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A. If A = 90° then cos A = 0, leaving a² = b² + c². If A is acute, cos A > 0 and the opposite side is shorter than Pythagoras would give; if A is obtuse, cos A < 0 and it is longer. The −2bc cos A term corrects for the angle not being a right angle.","k": ["cos 90|cos A = 0|cosine is zero","Pythagoras","acute|shorter","obtuse|longer"]}],"m-probability-gcse": [{"l": 2,"q": "A bag holds 3 red and 2 blue counters. Explain why the probability that the second counter drawn is red depends on whether the first is replaced.","a": "With replacement the bag is the same for the second draw, so P(red) = 3/5 each time: the draws are independent. Without replacement the contents change: if the first was red, P(second red) = 2/4; if blue, 3/4. The second draw depends on the first, so the second branches of the tree change.","k": ["replaced|replacement|put back","independent","dependent|depends|changes","2/4|3/4","tree"]}],"m-statistics": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the median is often a better average than the mean for house prices in a town.","a": "House prices usually include a few very expensive houses (outliers), which pull the mean up and make it unrepresentative of a typical house. The median is the middle value and is not affected by how extreme the largest values are, so it represents a typical price better. The distribution is positively skewed.","k": ["outliers|extreme values|very expensive","pulls the mean|affected|mean is pulled","middle value|middle","skew|skewed"]}],"m-vectors": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the dot product of two perpendicular vectors is zero, and how the dot product gives the angle between two vectors.","a": "a·b = |a||b| cos θ and cos 90° = 0, so perpendicular vectors give zero. Conversely, computing a·b from components (a₁b₁ + a₂b₂ + a₃b₃) and dividing by |a||b| gives cos θ, so θ = cos⁻¹(a·b ÷ |a||b|).","k": ["cos θ|cosine","cos 90|perpendicular","components","inverse cos|cos⁻¹|arccos"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain what the vector AB = b − a means, and why it is not a − b.","a": "a and b are the position vectors of A and B from the origin O. To go from A to B, go from A back to O (−a) and then from O to B (+b): AB = −a + b = b − a. The vector a − b goes from B to A, the opposite direction.","k": ["position vector|position vectors|origin","back to O|−a|from A to O","b − a|b - a","opposite direction|from B to A"]}],"m-differentiation": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain what a derivative means geometrically, and why it has to be defined as a limit.","a": "The derivative f′(x) is the gradient of the tangent to the curve at x: the instantaneous rate of change. A chord from x to x + h has gradient (f(x + h) − f(x))/h, but at a single point there is no run to divide by. Letting h → 0 makes the chord approach the tangent, giving the gradient at the point.","k": ["tangent","chord","limit|h → 0|h tends to 0","rate of change|gradient"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why the chain rule is needed to differentiate sin(x²), and why the result is 2x cos(x²).","a": "sin(x²) is a composite: an inner function u = x² inside an outer function sin u. A small change in x changes u at the rate du/dx = 2x, and each change in u changes sin u at the rate cos u. The rates multiply: dy/dx = (dy/du)(du/dx) = cos(x²) × 2x.","k": ["composite|function of a function|inner","chain rule|multiply","2x","cos(x²)|cos(x^2)|cos x^2"]}],"m-integration": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the integral of x³ from −1 to 1 is 0, even though the curve encloses some area.","a": "An integral gives signed area: area above the axis counts as positive and below as negative. x³ is an odd function, so the region from −1 to 0, below the axis, exactly mirrors the region from 0 to 1 above it, and they cancel. The total geometric area is 2 × ¼ = ½.","k": ["signed area|negative","below the axis|below","odd function|odd|symmetric","cancel"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain the idea behind the fundamental theorem of calculus: why does the area under f from a to b equal F(b) − F(a) when F′ = f?","a": "Let A(x) be the area under f from a to x. Increasing x by a small h adds a thin strip of area about f(x)h, so A′(x) = f(x): the area function is an antiderivative of f. Two antiderivatives differ by a constant, so A(x) = F(x) − F(a) (because A(a) = 0), and the area up to b is F(b) − F(a).","k": ["area function|A(x)","strip|f(x)h","antiderivative|A′(x) = f(x)|derivative of the area","constant|F(a)"]}],"m-exp-log": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why eˣ is its own derivative, and why this makes e the natural base for growth.","a": "For any base, d/dx aˣ = aˣ × lim (aʰ − 1)/h, so the growth rate is proportional to the current value. e is the one base for which that constant equals 1, so d/dx eˣ = eˣ. Any exponential can then be written e^{kx}, where k is directly the growth rate per unit x, which is why natural growth and decay laws use e.","k": ["proportional|current value","constant equals 1|constant is 1|limit","only base|unique|one base","growth rate|rate|e^"]}],"m-ode1": [{"l": 2,"q": "Using dy/dx = 2x, explain why a first-order differential equation needs an initial condition to give a single solution.","a": "dy/dx = 2x gives only the gradient at each x. Integrating gives y = x² + C, a whole family of curves (vertical shifts of each other) with the same gradients. An initial condition such as y(0) = 3 picks the one curve through that point, fixing C = 3.","k": ["gradient|slope","family","constant|+ C|arbitrary constant","picks|fixes|one curve|through that point"]}],"m-circles": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why a tangent to a circle is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact.","a": "The tangent touches the circle at one point P, and every other point of the tangent lies outside the circle, so P is the point of the tangent closest to the centre O. The shortest distance from a point to a line is along the perpendicular, so OP is perpendicular to the tangent.","k": ["closest|shortest distance","outside","perpendicular","one point|touches"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the perpendicular bisector of any chord passes through the centre of the circle.","a": "Both ends of the chord lie on the circle, so both are a distance r from the centre. The set of all points equidistant from two points is the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining them, so the centre lies on it.","k": ["equidistant|same distance","radius|r","perpendicular bisector","both ends|endpoints|ends"]}],"m-radians": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why sin θ ≈ θ for small θ only when θ is measured in radians.","a": "In radians the arc length on a unit circle is just θ (s = rθ). For a small angle the arc is almost straight and nearly equal to the perpendicular height, sin θ, so sin θ ≈ θ. In degrees the arc length is (π/180)θ, so sin θ ≈ πθ/180 instead. Radians are the unit that makes the derivative of sin at 0 equal to 1.","k": ["arc length|s = rθ|arc","unit circle","almost straight|nearly straight|height","degrees|π/180"]}],"m-series": [{"l": 2,"q": "Use the geometric series formula to explain why 1 + ½ + ¼ + ⅛ + … has a finite sum but 1 + 1 + 1 + … does not.","a": "Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r). With common ratio r = ½, which is less than 1 in size, rⁿ → 0 as n → ∞, so S∞ = 1/(1 − ½) = 2: each term fills half of the remaining gap. With r = 1 (or any |r| ≥ 1), rⁿ does not tend to 0, the terms do not shrink, and the partial sums grow without bound.","k": ["tends to 0|tends to zero|→ 0","less than 1|ratio","2","without bound|diverges|infinite"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why the binomial expansion of (1 + x)ⁿ for non-integer n is an infinite series that is valid only for |x| < 1.","a": "For a positive integer n the coefficients n(n − 1)…(n − k + 1)/k! eventually contain a factor 0, so the series stops. For non-integer n no factor is ever zero, so the series never ends. It converges only if its terms eventually shrink; the ratio of successive terms tends to −x, so it needs |x| < 1 (for example 1/(1 + x) = 1 − x + x² − … fails at x = 2).","k": ["factor 0|zero factor|becomes zero|stops","infinite|never ends","converge|converges","ratio|−x"]}],"m-polynomials": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain the factor theorem: why does f(a) = 0 mean that (x − a) is a factor of f(x)?","a": "Dividing f(x) by (x − a) gives f(x) = (x − a)q(x) + R, with a constant remainder R. Putting x = a gives f(a) = R. So if f(a) = 0, the remainder is zero and (x − a) divides f(x) exactly.","k": ["remainder","f(a) = R|substitute|put x = a","exactly|no remainder|remainder is zero","quotient|q(x)"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain how a repeated root shows up on the graph of a polynomial, and why.","a": "If (x − a)² is a factor, then near x = a, f(x) ≈ k(x − a)², which does not change sign: the graph touches the x-axis and turns back instead of crossing it, with a turning point on the axis. Both f(a) and f′(a) are zero there. With (x − a)³ the graph crosses with a flat point of inflection.","k": ["touches|touch","does not change sign|no sign change|same sign","turning point|stationary|f′(a) = 0","squared|square|(x − a)²"]}],"m-statics": [{"l": 2,"q": "A box rests on a rough slope without sliding. Explain why the friction force on it is not necessarily μR.","a": "μR is the maximum (limiting) friction. Static friction adjusts to whatever is needed to prevent sliding, up to that limit. Here it equals the component of weight down the slope, mg sin θ, which may be less than μR. Only when the box is on the point of slipping is F = μR.","k": ["maximum|limiting|at most","adjusts|whatever is needed|up to","mg sin θ|component of weight|down the slope","point of slipping|about to slip|on the point"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why, for a particle on a slope, it is usually best to resolve forces parallel and perpendicular to the slope.","a": "The normal reaction is perpendicular to the slope, friction acts along it, and any acceleration is along it. Resolving in these directions means only the weight has to be split into components (mg sin θ and mg cos θ), and the perpendicular equation gives R directly, with no acceleration term.","k": ["normal reaction|reaction","friction","acceleration","components|mg sin θ|mg cos θ"]}],"m-kinematics-calc": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why you cannot use the suvat equations when the acceleration is a = 6t, and what you do instead.","a": "The suvat equations are derived assuming constant acceleration, but a = 6t changes with time. Instead integrate: v = ∫a dt = 3t² + c, then s = ∫v dt, using the initial conditions to find the constants.","k": ["constant acceleration|not constant","integrate|integration","3t²|3t^2","initial conditions|constant"]}],"m-newton-mech": [{"l": 2,"q": "Two particles joined by a light, inextensible string pass over a smooth pulley. Explain why the tension is the same on both sides and why the particles have the same size of acceleration.","a": "The string is light, so the net force on any piece of it must be zero, and a smooth pulley has no friction to change the tension, so the tension is the same throughout. The string is inextensible, so when one particle moves a distance x the other moves x too; their speeds and accelerations are equal in size.","k": ["light|massless","smooth|no friction","inextensible|does not stretch","same acceleration|same distance|equal"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why a particle on a rough slope accelerates down it more slowly than g sin θ, and when it stays at rest.","a": "Down the slope the net force is mg sin θ minus friction μR = μmg cos θ, so a = g(sin θ − μ cos θ), less than g sin θ. If tan θ ≤ μ, the maximum friction is at least mg sin θ, so friction can balance the weight component and the particle stays at rest.","k": ["friction","μ cos θ|mg cos θ","tan θ","rest|stays still"]}],"m-momentum-mech": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why a cricketer moves his hands backwards when catching a fast ball.","a": "The impulse needed to stop the ball equals its change in momentum, which is fixed: FΔt = Δp. Moving the hands back increases the time over which the ball is stopped, so the average force on the hands is smaller.","k": ["impulse","change in momentum|momentum","longer time|increases the time|more time","smaller force|less force|reduces the force"]}],"m-work-energy-mech": [{"l": 2,"q": "State the work–energy principle and explain why it often finds a speed more quickly than F = ma and suvat.","a": "The net work done on a particle equals its change in kinetic energy. When forces vary or the path is curved, finding the acceleration at every point is hard, but the work done by each force can be added (gravity's work depends only on height), so the final speed follows from ½mv² − ½mu² without knowing the time or the acceleration.","k": ["net work|work done","change in kinetic energy|kinetic energy","path|height|varying","time|acceleration not needed|without"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the work done by gravity on a particle sliding down a smooth curved track depends only on the height it drops.","a": "Work = force × displacement along the force. Gravity is constant and vertical, so only the vertical part of each small displacement counts: δW = mg δh. Adding all the steps gives mgh for the total vertical drop, whatever the shape of the track (gravity is conservative). The normal reaction is perpendicular to the motion and does no work.","k": ["vertical","mgh","shape|path|conservative","normal reaction|perpendicular|no work"]}],"m-data": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain how coding the data with y = (x − 100)/5 changes the mean and the standard deviation, and why.","a": "Subtracting 100 shifts every value equally, so the mean shifts by 100 but the spread is unchanged. Dividing by 5 scales every distance from the mean by 1/5, so the mean and the standard deviation are both divided by 5: new mean (x̄ − 100)/5, new standard deviation σ/5.","k": ["shift|subtract","spread|unchanged|not affected","divided by 5|scales|1/5","standard deviation|σ/5"]}],"m-combinatorics": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why there are ¹⁰C₃ = 120 ways to choose a committee of 3 from 10 people, but ¹⁰P₃ = 720 ways to choose a chair, a secretary and a treasurer.","a": "For officers, order matters: 10 choices for chair, then 9 for secretary, then 8 for treasurer, 720 in all. A plain committee ignores order: each group of 3 people appears in 3! = 6 orders, so there are 720 ÷ 6 = 120 committees.","k": ["order matters|order","720|10 × 9 × 8","3!|divide by 6|6 orders","120"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why the letters of BANANA can be arranged in 6!/(3!2!) = 60 ways.","a": "If all six letters were different there would be 6! = 720 arrangements. But swapping the three A's among themselves (3! = 6 ways) or the two N's (2! = 2 ways) gives the same word, so each distinct word was counted 3! × 2! = 12 times. 720 ÷ 12 = 60.","k": ["6!|720","identical|repeated|same letter","3!|2!","counted|overcount|divide"]}],"m-probability-al": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain the difference between mutually exclusive and independent events, and why two events with non-zero probabilities cannot be both.","a": "Mutually exclusive events cannot happen together: P(A ∩ B) = 0. Independent events do not affect each other's probability: P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B). If P(A) and P(B) are both non-zero, P(A)P(B) > 0, so the events cannot also have P(A ∩ B) = 0. Exclusive events are in fact strongly dependent: if A happens, B certainly does not.","k": ["cannot happen together|both at once|P(A ∩ B) = 0","P(A)P(B)|multiply|do not affect","non-zero|greater than 0|> 0","dependent"]}],"m-discrete-rv": [{"l": 2,"q": "State the conditions for a binomial model, and explain why counting heads in 10 coin tosses fits it but counting aces among 5 cards dealt without replacement does not.","a": "A binomial model needs a fixed number of trials n, two outcomes per trial, independent trials and a constant probability p. Coin tosses meet all four. Dealing cards without replacement changes the probability of an ace after each card and makes the draws dependent, so p is not constant (that is a hypergeometric situation).","k": ["fixed number of trials|fixed n|fixed number","independent","constant probability|constant p|same probability","two outcomes|success|failure","without replacement|changes"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why Var(X) = E(X²) − [E(X)]² can never be negative.","a": "Var(X) = E[(X − μ)²] is the average of squared distances from the mean, and a square is never negative, so the average cannot be negative. Expanding (X − μ)² = X² − 2μX + μ² and taking expectations gives E(X²) − μ², the same quantity. It is zero only when X always equals μ.","k": ["squared distances|square|(X − μ)²","never negative|non-negative|cannot be negative","expand|expanding","zero only|constant|always equals"]}],"m-normal": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why we standardise a normal variable using Z = (X − μ)/σ.","a": "Every normal distribution has the same shape once you shift it by its mean and measure distances in standard deviations. Z = (X − μ)/σ turns any N(μ, σ²) into N(0, 1), so one table or calculator function of standard normal probabilities serves for all of them. Z says how many standard deviations X is from the mean.","k": ["same shape","subtract the mean|shift","standard deviations|divide by σ","N(0, 1)|standard normal|one table"]},{"l": 2,"q": "For a continuous normal variable, explain why P(X = 5) = 0 while P(4.5 < X < 5.5) is not zero.","a": "For a continuous variable probability is area under the density curve. A single value is a line of zero width, so its area is zero. An interval has positive width and the density is positive there, so its area is positive.","k": ["area","zero width|width","interval|range","density|pdf|curve"]}],"m-poisson": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain the conditions under which the number of calls to a help-line per hour can be modelled by a Poisson distribution.","a": "Calls must happen singly (never two at exactly the same instant), independently of each other, at random, and at a constant average rate λ per hour. Then the count in a fixed interval is Po(λ). The model fails if, for example, calls bunch up after a news story or the rate changes through the day.","k": ["independent|independently","random","constant rate|constant average rate|constant","singly|one at a time"]}],"m-linear-combinations": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why Var(X − Y) = Var(X) + Var(Y) for independent X and Y, rather than Var(X) − Var(Y).","a": "Variance measures spread, and combining two independent uncertain quantities can only add uncertainty: Y's variability adds to X's whether you add Y or subtract it. Formally Var(X − Y) = Var(X) + (−1)²Var(Y) = Var(X) + Var(Y), because the coefficient −1 is squared.","k": ["spread|uncertainty|variability","independent","squared|square|(−1)²","add|adds"]}],"m-continuous-rv": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why a probability density function can be greater than 1 at some points even though probabilities cannot exceed 1.","a": "f(x) is probability per unit of x, not a probability. Only the area under f over an interval is a probability. If the variable is spread over a short range, such as uniform on [0, 0.5], then f = 2 there, but the total area is still 2 × 0.5 = 1.","k": ["per unit|density","area","total area|integrates to 1|area is 1","short|narrow|uniform"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain how the cumulative distribution function F(x) is related to the pdf f(x), and how you find the median from it.","a": "F(x) = P(X ≤ x) is the area under f to the left of x, the integral of f from the lower limit up to x. Differentiating F gives back f. The median m splits the probability in half, so it solves F(m) = 0.5.","k": ["area to the left|integral|integrate","P(X ≤ x)","differentiate|derivative|f = F′","0.5|half"]}],"m-sampling": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why a larger random sample gives a more precise estimate of a population mean, and why it does not cure a biased sampling method.","a": "The sample mean has standard error σ/√n, so its spread about μ shrinks as n grows (four times the sample halves the error). A biased method, such as surveying only people at a gym about how much they exercise, is centred on the wrong value; a bigger sample just gives a more precise estimate of the wrong thing.","k": ["standard error|σ/√n","√n|root n|square root","bias|biased","wrong value|wrong thing|centred on the wrong"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain what a 95% confidence interval means, and what it does not mean.","a": "If we repeated the sampling many times and built an interval each time by the same method, about 95% of those intervals would contain the true mean μ. It does not mean there is a 95% probability that μ lies in this particular interval: μ is fixed, and this interval either contains it or does not.","k": ["repeated|many samples|many times","95% of","contain the true mean|contain μ|true value","fixed|not a probability"]}],"m-hypothesis": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain what a p-value is, and why a p-value of 0.03 does not mean there is a 3% chance that H₀ is true.","a": "The p-value is the probability, assuming H₀ is true, of getting a result at least as extreme as the one observed. It describes the data given H₀, not H₀ given the data. The probability that H₀ is true would also need prior information (Bayes' theorem).","k": ["assuming H₀|assuming the null|given H₀|if H₀","at least as extreme|as extreme","data given|not H₀ given","Bayes|prior"]}],"m-roots-poly": [{"l": 2,"q": "For a quadratic ax² + bx + c with roots α and β, explain why α + β = −b/a and αβ = c/a.","a": "Because α and β are the roots, the quadratic factorises as ax² + bx + c = a(x − α)(x − β); expanding gives a[x² − (α + β)x + αβ]. Comparing coefficients: −a(α + β) = b, so α + β = −b/a; and aαβ = c, so αβ = c/a.","k": ["a(x − α)(x − β)|factorised|factorized|factor","expand|expanding","compare|comparing coefficients","−b/a|-b/a","c/a"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Explain how to find α² + β² + γ² for the roots of a cubic without finding the roots.","a": "Square the sum: (α + β + γ)² = α² + β² + γ² + 2(αβ + βγ + γα). So α² + β² + γ² = (Σα)² − 2Σαβ, and both Σα = −b/a and Σαβ = c/a come straight from the coefficients.","k": ["square the sum|squaring|(α + β + γ)²","2Σαβ|cross terms|2(αβ + βγ + γα)","−b/a|-b/a","c/a|coefficients"]}],"m-matrix-transforms": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the columns of a 2 × 2 transformation matrix are the images of the unit vectors i and j.","a": "Multiplying the matrix by (1, 0) picks out its first column, and by (0, 1) its second. So column 1 is where i goes and column 2 is where j goes. Because the transformation is linear, every vector xi + yj goes to x(image of i) + y(image of j), so these two columns determine the whole transformation.","k": ["(1, 0)|unit vector|i","first column|picks out","linear","every vector|any vector|determine"]}],"m-polar": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the area enclosed by a polar curve is ½∫r² dθ.","a": "Split the region into thin sectors from the origin, each with angle dθ and radius r(θ). A thin sector of a circle with angle dθ has area ½r² dθ. Adding them and taking the limit gives A = ½∫r² dθ between the limiting angles.","k": ["sector|sectors","½r²|area of a sector|sector area","dθ|small angle","add|sum|limit"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the polar curve r = 2a cos θ is a circle.","a": "Multiply by r: r² = 2ar cos θ, so x² + y² = 2ax. Completing the square gives (x − a)² + y² = a², a circle of radius a centred at (a, 0), passing through the origin.","k": ["multiply by r|r²","x² + y²|x^2 + y^2","completing the square|complete the square","radius a|centre|centred"]}],"m-planes": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the vector equation of a plane can be written r·n = d.","a": "Take a point A on the plane with position vector a, and a normal n perpendicular to the plane. For any point R on the plane, AR = r − a lies in the plane, so (r − a)·n = 0. Hence r·n = a·n, a constant d.","k": ["normal","perpendicular","lies in the plane|(r − a)·n = 0|r − a","constant|a·n"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the cross product of the normals of two planes gives the direction of their line of intersection.","a": "The line lies in both planes, so its direction is perpendicular to both normals n₁ and n₂. The cross product n₁ × n₂ is perpendicular to both of its factors, so it points along the line (as long as the planes are not parallel, so that the product is non-zero).","k": ["both planes|in both","perpendicular","cross product","not parallel|non-zero"]}],"m-hyperbolic": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why cosh²x − sinh²x = 1, and why the point (cosh t, sinh t) lies on a hyperbola.","a": "cosh x = (eˣ + e⁻ˣ)/2 and sinh x = (eˣ − e⁻ˣ)/2. Squaring and expanding, cosh²x − sinh²x = [(e^{2x} + 2 + e^{−2x}) − (e^{2x} − 2 + e^{−2x})]/4 = 4/4 = 1. So x = cosh t, y = sinh t satisfies x² − y² = 1, a rectangular hyperbola, just as (cos t, sin t) satisfies x² + y² = 1.","k": ["exponentials|definition|e^","expand|square","x² − y² = 1|x^2 - y^2 = 1|x² - y² = 1","hyperbola"]}],"m-fm-projectiles": [{"l": 2,"q": "Ignoring air resistance, explain why launch angles of 30° and 60° give the same range on level ground.","a": "The range is R = u² sin 2θ/g, and sin 60° = sin 120°, so 2θ = 60° and 2θ = 120° give the same range. In general θ and 90° − θ give equal ranges: the steeper shot stays in the air longer but moves forward more slowly, and the two effects exactly balance.","k": ["sin 2θ|R = u² sin 2θ","sin 60|sin 120|same sine","complementary|90° − θ|90 − θ","longer|slower|balance|compensate"]}],"m-rigid-equilibrium": [{"l": 2,"q": "A ladder leans against a smooth wall on a rough floor. Explain why it is more likely to slip when a person climbs higher up it.","a": "Taking moments about the foot of the ladder, the wall's reaction must balance the moments of the ladder's weight and the person's weight. As the person climbs, their moment about the foot grows, so the wall's horizontal reaction grows. Horizontal equilibrium needs the friction at the floor to equal that reaction, so the friction needed grows; once it would exceed μR, the ladder slips.","k": ["moments|moment about the foot|moment about the base","wall reaction|reaction from the wall|wall","friction","μR|limiting|maximum"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why a hole cut from a lamina can be treated as a “negative mass” when finding the centre of mass.","a": "The centre of mass is the mass-weighted average position, x̄ = Σmx/Σm. The full plate is the remaining shape plus the cut-out piece, so the moments of the full plate equal the moments of the remaining shape plus those of the hole. Rearranging, the remaining shape's moments are the full plate's minus the hole's: the hole enters with negative mass.","k": ["weighted average|Σmx","full plate|whole|complete","minus|subtract","moments"]}],"m-vertical-circle": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why a bucket of water swung in a vertical circle must move fast enough at the top for the water not to fall out.","a": "At the top the net downward force on the water must equal mv²/r to keep it on the circle. The weight mg and the push of the bucket provide this. If v² < gr, gravity alone is more than the required centripetal force; the bucket would have to pull the water upwards, which it cannot, so the water leaves the circle. So v² ≥ gr at the top.","k": ["mv²/r|centripetal","weight|mg","gr|√(gr)","cannot pull|push|reaction"]},{"l": 3,"q": "A particle on a light rod and a particle on a string are each whirled in a vertical circle. Explain why the condition for completing the circle differs.","a": "A rod can push as well as pull, so the particle only needs enough energy to reach the top: v ≥ 0 there, i.e. u² ≥ 4gr at the bottom. A string can only pull (T ≥ 0), so at the top gravity must not exceed the required centripetal force: v² ≥ gr there, i.e. u² ≥ 5gr at the bottom.","k": ["rod can push|push|thrust","string can only pull|tension|T ≥ 0","4gr","5gr"]}],"m-elastic": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the elastic potential energy in a stretched string is λx²/2l rather than (λx/l) × x.","a": "The tension is not constant during the stretch; it rises steadily from 0 to λx/l. The work done is the area under the tension–extension graph, a triangle: ½ × x × λx/l = λx²/2l. Multiplying the final tension by x would assume the full tension acted for the whole extension.","k": ["not constant|increases|varies|rises","area under|area","triangle|½|half","final tension|whole extension"]},{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why two identical springs in series are softer than one spring, but two in parallel are stiffer.","a": "In series each spring carries the full load, so each stretches as much as a single spring would; the total extension doubles and the stiffness halves. In parallel the load is shared, so each carries half and stretches half as much; the pair is twice as stiff.","k": ["full load|same tension|same force","extension doubles|stretches twice|twice the extension","shared|half the load|half","twice as stiff|stiffer|stiffness doubles"]}],"m-variable-force": [{"l": 2,"q": "A force depends on position, F = −kx. Explain why you write a = v dv/dx, and how this leads to energy conservation.","a": "By the chain rule, a = dv/dt = (dv/dx)(dx/dt) = v dv/dx, which lets you integrate with respect to x. Then mv dv/dx = −kx gives ∫mv dv = −∫kx dx, so ½mv² + ½kx² = constant: kinetic plus elastic energy is conserved.","k": ["chain rule|dx/dt","v dv/dx","integrate|integration","energy|½mv² + ½kx²|constant"]}],"m-restitution": [{"l": 2,"q": "A smooth ball hits a smooth wall at an angle. Explain why its velocity component parallel to the wall is unchanged while the perpendicular component is reduced.","a": "The wall is smooth, so the impulse it gives acts along the normal, perpendicular to the wall, with no friction along it. No impulse parallel to the wall means no change in that component of momentum, so the parallel velocity is unchanged. Newton's law of restitution applies along the line of impact: the perpendicular component reverses and is multiplied by e.","k": ["smooth|no friction","impulse|normal","unchanged|parallel component","line of impact|perpendicular|restitution"]}],"m-partial": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the gradient vector ∇f is perpendicular to the contours (level curves) of f.","a": "Along a contour f is constant, so for a small step dr along it, df = ∇f · dr = 0. A zero dot product means ∇f is perpendicular to every direction along the contour. ∇f points in the direction of steepest increase, straight uphill across the contours.","k": ["constant","dot product|∇f · dr","zero|= 0","steepest|uphill"]}],"m-matrices": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain, in terms of what it does to space, why a matrix with determinant zero has no inverse.","a": "The determinant is the factor by which the transformation scales areas (or volumes). A determinant of zero means it squashes space into a lower dimension, a line or a point, so different vectors are sent to the same image. Information is lost and the map cannot be undone, so there is no inverse.","k": ["scale factor|scales area|area|volume","squashes|collapses|lower dimension|flattens","same image|many-to-one|not one-to-one","cannot be undone|no inverse|lost"]}],"m-ode2": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain how the sign of b² − 4mk decides whether the damped oscillator mx″ + bx′ + kx = 0 oscillates.","a": "Trying x = e^{λt} gives mλ² + bλ + k = 0, so λ = (−b ± √(b² − 4mk))/2m. If b² < 4mk the roots are complex and the solution is e^{−bt/2m} times sines and cosines: underdamped oscillation. If b² > 4mk both roots are real and negative: overdamped, a slow return with no oscillation. b² = 4mk is critical damping, the quickest return without overshooting.","k": ["e^|auxiliary|characteristic","complex","real","critical"]}],"m-svd": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why the least-squares solution of Ax ≈ b satisfies AᵀAx = Aᵀb.","a": "We want the vector Ax in the column space of A that is closest to b. The closest point is the orthogonal projection, so the residual b − Ax must be perpendicular to every column of A: Aᵀ(b − Ax) = 0. Rearranging gives the normal equations AᵀAx = Aᵀb.","k": ["closest|minimise|minimize","column space","residual|b − Ax","perpendicular|orthogonal","normal equations|Aᵀ(b − Ax) = 0"]}],"m-theorems": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain the idea behind the divergence theorem: why does the total outward flux through a closed surface equal the volume integral of div F?","a": "Split the volume into tiny cubes. div F at a point is the net outward flux per unit volume from a tiny cube there. Adding over all the cubes, the flux through every internal face is counted twice with opposite signs and cancels, leaving only the flux through the outer surface.","k": ["tiny cubes|small volumes|split","per unit volume|outflow","internal faces|cancel","outer surface|boundary|outside"]}],"m-fourier-series": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why each Fourier coefficient can be found on its own by multiplying by sin(nx) and integrating.","a": "Over one period, ∫sin(mx) sin(nx) dx = 0 for m ≠ n, and likewise for cosines and mixed products: the functions are orthogonal. Multiplying the whole series by sin(nx) and integrating makes every other term vanish, leaving only the sin(nx) term: bₙπ = ∫f(x) sin(nx) dx over −π to π. Each coefficient is the projection of f onto one basis function.","k": ["orthogonal|orthogonality","zero|= 0","every other term|all other terms|vanish","coefficient|projection"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why a square wave's Fourier coefficients fall off like 1/n while a triangle wave's fall off like 1/n², and what this means visually.","a": "Integrating by parts links the coefficients of f to those of f′ divided by n. A square wave has jumps, which need strong high harmonics to build, so its coefficients decay only as 1/n, and partial sums overshoot near the jumps (the Gibbs phenomenon). A triangle wave is continuous with only kinks; its derivative is a square wave, so its coefficients are a further factor n smaller, 1/n², and its series converges faster and looks smooth sooner.","k": ["jumps|discontinuous|discontinuity","kinks|continuous","integrate by parts|derivative","Gibbs|overshoot","faster|converges"]}],"m-random-walks": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why a random walker's typical distance from the start grows as √N rather than N after N steps.","a": "The steps are independent with mean zero, so the displacement x = Σsᵢ has mean zero. Squaring, ⟨x²⟩ = Σ⟨sᵢ²⟩ + Σ⟨sᵢsⱼ⟩, and the cross terms average to zero because different steps are independent, leaving ⟨x²⟩ = Na². The root-mean-square distance is √N a: the steps partly cancel.","k": ["independent","mean zero|average zero","cross terms|cancel","mean square|⟨x²⟩","√N|root N|square root"]}],"m-finite-difference": [{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why making the step size h smaller does not keep improving a finite-difference estimate of a derivative.","a": "Truncation error from the Taylor series shrinks with h (∝ h for a forward difference, h² for a central one). But f(x + h) − f(x) subtracts two nearly equal numbers, each carrying rounding error of about ε|f|, and the result is divided by h, so the rounding error grows like ε/h. The total error is smallest at an intermediate h (about √ε for a forward difference) and grows again for smaller h.","k": ["truncation","rounding","ε/h|divided by h|divide by h","nearly equal|subtract|cancellation","intermediate|optimum|√ε"]}],"m-monte-carlo": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain why Monte Carlo integration beats grid methods for integrals in many dimensions.","a": "The Monte Carlo error is σ/√N, set by the number of samples whatever the dimension. A grid with n points per dimension needs nᵈ points, so for a fixed total N the spacing is N^{−1/d} and a grid method of order k has error falling only as N^{−k/d}, which becomes terrible as d grows: the curse of dimensionality.","k": ["1/√N|σ/√N|√N","dimension|independent of dimension","grid points|nᵈ|exponentially","curse of dimensionality"]}],"m-nonlinear": [{"l": 2,"q": "Explain how you find the stability of a fixed point of dx/dt = f(x) by linearising.","a": "At a fixed point x*, f(x*) = 0, so for a small deviation η = x − x*, dη/dt ≈ f′(x*)η, giving η ∝ e^{f′(x*)t}. If f′(x*) < 0 deviations decay (stable); if f′(x*) > 0 they grow (unstable). If f′(x*) = 0 the linearisation is inconclusive and higher-order terms decide.","k": ["small deviation|perturbation|small displacement","derivative|f′","decay|stable","grow|unstable","inconclusive|higher-order"]},{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why chaos cannot occur in a two-dimensional autonomous system of ODEs but can in three dimensions.","a": "Trajectories of an autonomous system cannot cross (solutions are unique). In the plane a bounded trajectory is hemmed in by its own path, and the Poincaré–Bendixson theorem says it must approach a fixed point or a periodic orbit (limit cycle), so there is no chaos. In 3D trajectories can pass over and under each other, allowing the stretching and folding of a strange attractor, as in the Lorenz system.","k": ["cannot cross|uniqueness|unique","Poincaré|Poincare|Bendixson","fixed point|limit cycle|periodic orbit","stretching|folding|strange attractor|Lorenz"]}],"m-jacobi": [{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why the exact period of a large-amplitude pendulum involves an elliptic integral, and why the Jacobi functions describe its motion.","a": "Energy conservation gives dθ/dt = √((2g/L)(cos θ − cos θ₀)), so the time is ∫dθ/√(cos θ − cos θ₀). With sin(θ/2) = k sin φ and k = sin(θ₀/2) this becomes K(k) = ∫₀^{π/2} dφ/√(1 − k² sin²φ), the complete elliptic integral, and T = 4√(L/g) K(k). Inverting the incomplete integral, to get angle as a function of time, gives sn: sin(θ/2) = k sn(√(g/L) t, k), just as inverting arcsin gives sin for small swings.","k": ["energy conservation|energy","elliptic integral|K(k)","invert|inverse|inverting","sn|Jacobi","k = sin(θ₀/2)|modulus"]}],"m-zeta": [{"l": 3,"q": "Explain why the Riemann zeta function appears in the condition for Bose–Einstein condensation, nλ³ = 2.612.","a": "The number of bosons in excited states is ∫g(E) dE/(e^{(E−μ)/kT} − 1). Expanding 1/(eˣ − 1) as a geometric series Σe^{−jx} and integrating term by term gives sums of 1/j^{3/2}, which is ζ(3/2) ≈ 2.612 once μ → 0 at the transition. This fixes the largest number that can sit in excited states; any extra particles must go into the ground state, which is condensation.","k": ["geometric series|expand|expanding","term by term","ζ(3/2)|2.612|zeta","μ → 0|chemical potential","ground state|maximum|largest number"]}]};
